Use the pure-SU(2) physical charge datum with monopole label $\gamma_m=(0,1)$ and dyon label $\gamma_d=(1,-1)$. Their source-normalized central charges are $z_m(u)=\sqrt2\,a_D(u)$ and $z_d(u)=\sqrt2\,(a(u)-a_D(u))$, and their chosen Dirac pairing is $\langle\gamma_m,\gamma_d\rangle_{\mathrm{DSZ}}=2$. For a prime power $q>1$, take the finite acyclic quiver with vertices $m,d$ and two arrows from $d$ to $m$:

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,baseline=(current bounding box.center)]
\node (m) at (0,0) {m};
\node (d) at (2,0) {d};
\draw[->] (d) to[bend left=18] node[above] {a} (m);
\draw[->] (d) to[bend right=18] node[below] {b} (m);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

The charge map sends the two vertex basis vectors to $\gamma_m,\gamma_d$; it matches the quiver skew Euler form because both pairings give $2$ on the ordered pair $(m,d)$. Suppose regular points $u_-,u_+\in U$ and a real phase rotation $\alpha$ are supplied such that both rotated central charges lie in $\mathbb H=\{z\in\mathbb C:\operatorname{Im}z>0\}$ at both points, with $\phi_m(u_-)<\phi_d(u_-)$ and $\phi_d(u_+)<\phi_m(u_+)$, where $\phi_i(u)=\arg(e^{-i\alpha}z_i(u))/\pi$. Among representations of dimension $(1,1)$, the nonzero arrow pairs have $q+1$ stable isomorphism classes, each with automorphism group of order $q-1$, while the zero pair is unstable; the skew-integrated stable-factor coefficient is therefore $(q+1)/(q-1)$ because the Euler self-pairing of $(1,1)$ is zero. At $u_+$, there is no semistable representation of that dimension. The complete finite-field Hall generators nevertheless agree for the two charge assignments. This rational groupoid weight is not a physical second-helicity supertrace. If all hypotheses of the A-page theorem “Conditional physical BPS wall crossing from an exact Hall bridge” hold for these points—including cone-supported integer-valued nonzero index support with phases defined at both points, completed charge-series topologies, and the exact continuous per-phase bridge—then the finite-field Hall equality transfers to the physical KS automorphisms; this example does not assert those additional inputs.
